# **On negotiating: When my bottom line price is more than you’re willing to pay**

###### February 19, 2014

\
\
So many things can matter more than price. At one of the previous companies I worked for, we increased our deal size by 79% while raising our actual price per seat by 46%. One of our methods was that we stopped defaulting to price as our primary tool for closing a sale. Instead, we took the time to listen to customers, understand what mattered most to them, and shaped deals that addressed their concerns. Here are three stories that illustrate some of our best deals.\
\
**No Blunder Down-Under**

Our account executive traveled to Australia in May and returned with a handshake. He had done a masterful job showing the benefits of our solution and formed great relationships. He had beaten back challenges from an incumbent competitor and built trust across the organization. The sales job had been stellar. Now it was time to get the deal closed, and he asked me to approve a modest price cut as an incentive to get it over the line by June. My only condition was this: any price reduction had to be the last thing we offered, after we learned everything it was going to take to get the deal closed.

As it turned out, there were a litany of things the customer wanted, all of which were defined over the most extensive negotiation I have ever conducted. The final contract was 162 pages long. It took over three months to negotiate; I was on the phone with the customer’s legal team for 42 hours, not counting the dozens of hours spent in internal meetings and prep. (We negotiated three pages of rules on how meetings were to be handled!)

Finally, the deal was done – at the exact price we had proposed five months before.

The lesson was pretty clear. As salespeople, we assumed that since price was the most important thing to *us*, it would also be the most motivating item for our *customer*. But the customer was satisfied with the price. They had things that mattered more to them, defined throughout a nearly 30,000 word contract. And once we had run the legal gauntlet, the negotiation was successfully concluded.

**A Phoenix from the Ashes**

We flew to Phoenix to negotiate a deal. The Senior Vice President of Sales held our quote in his hand. “We don’t have any money to cover this, so we can’t buy anything from you. It will be a stretch even to renew the subscriptions for the seats we already have.” [Tense pause.]

“Ok,” I said. “Tell me what you guys like about our product and why it’s important to you.” They explained how we made them more efficient, how the engineers were happier when they used our product, and how they saw us as key to offering a multi-product, integrated solution to their end-customers. It was clear that our sales executive had done a great job selling the value and recruiting coaches and advocates. He had gotten the SVP of Sales in the room to meet with me, so I was pretty sure they hadn’t dragged us to Phoenix just to tell us to go away.

“So let’s talk about what’s in your interests,” I said. “Give me some insight into how your budgeting works and when you will have the capacity to invest. Then we can talk creatively about commercial terms that can work for both of us.”

As it happened, their entire structure was based on cash. The price mattered, but the cash flow was key. It was easier for them to spend $10 next month than $1 this month. Once we understood that, we knew we could combine a staged purchase with some flexible payment terms. Our customer got the software he wanted at commercial terms that addressed all of his needs. We got the deal at the price we wanted (and all within our fiscal year) and our sales guy got his full commission.

**African Safari**

We were ready for part two of the two-part transaction we had set up a year before. The problem was this: our implementation was behind schedule, and our sponsor was not willing to “front” the money for all of his regions, which would ultimately be billed back for the solution. The hard milestones in the contract seemed to give us little breathing room. We were left with a terrible choice: either (1) cave on price beyond our comfort zone or (2) sell a deal in a way that limited future adoption and hurt the long-term business.

When we finally got to the bottom of things, their problem wasn’t money but time. We needed time to prove the solution to each of the regions, and then we could unlock the money. We chose an innovative option; we extended their option three months and gave them the right to “buy up” to the deal everyone wanted in the first place. We were able to capture the full value of the deal we wanted (with a little patience), and the customer got the breathing room they needed to complete the transaction.

**For More Reading**

I have been rereading [***Getting to Yes***](http://www.amazon.com/Getting-Yes-Negotiating-Agreement-ebook/dp/B0051SDM5Q/ref=tmm_kin_title_0?ie=UTF8&qid=1365788942&sr=8-1), for over thirty years one of the most important books on negotiation. Avoiding positional negotiation (and the stalemate where *my* bottom line exceeds the most *you’re* willing to spend) is one of the keys to arriving at win-win solutions. These three deals illustrate how a little creativity can get you out of the positional game, even when there seems to be little to negotiate except price.
